$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"a","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">None</p>\n"},{"id":"b","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">Exactly 1</p>\n"},{"id":"c","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">Exactly 3</p>\n"},{"id":"d","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">Infinitely many</p>\n"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, NULL, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is correct. Subtracting 4 from each side of the given equation yields <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/550b352c/img_001_177378ca.png" alt="6 equals 2 x"></span>, or <span class="italic"><span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/550b352c/img_002_6ec6a793.png" alt="x equals 3"></span></span>, so the equation has a unique solution of <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/550b352c/img_003_6ec6a793.png" alt="x equals 3"></span>.<p>Choice A is incorrect. Since 3 is a value of <span class="italic">x</span> that satisfies the given equation, the equation has at least 1 solution. Choice C is incorrect. Linear equations can have 0, 1, or infinitely many solutions; no linear equation has exactly 3 solutions. Choice D is incorrect. If a linear equation has infinitely many solutions, it can be reduced to <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/550b352c/img_004_b5f3047f.png" alt="0 equals 0"></span>. This equation reduces to <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/550b352c/img_005_6ec6a793.png" alt="x equals 3"></span>, so there is only 1 solution.</p><p>&nbsp;</p></p>
