parenthesis seven halves x plus 6 y right parenthesis plus left parenthesis five halves x plus 6 y right parenthesis\"><mfenced><mrow><mfrac><mn>7</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>6</mn><mi>y</mi></mrow></mfenced><mo>+</mo><mfenced><mrow><mfrac><mn>5</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>6</mn><mi>y</mi></mrow></mfenced></math>, or <math alttext=\"6 x plus 12 y\"><mrow>\n<mrow>\n<mn>6</mn>\n<mi>x</mi>\n</mrow>\n<mo>+</mo>\n<mrow>\n<mn>12</mn>\n<mi>y</mi>\n</mrow>\n</mrow>\n</math>, not the value of <math alttext=\"StartFraction 17 Over 2 EndFraction x plus 18 y\"><mfrac><mn>17</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>18</mn><mi>y</mi></math>.</p>","externalid":"b0266b5e-6f26-496c-81ea-3108b31c0bc6","correct_answer":["D"]},"createDate":1743430554985,"updateDate":1743430554985}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1743430554985, 1743430554985),
    ($SATQ$571174f3$SATQ$, $SATQ$f55f83d0-fd97-488f-9f5c-014bc1e34d9c$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ$Algebra$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, $SATQ$H.D.$SATQ$, $SATQ$Systems of two linear equations in two variables$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$spr$SATQ$, NULL, $SATQ$<p style="text-align: center;"><math alttext="two fifths x plus seven fifths y equals two sevenths"><mfrac>
	<mn>2</mn>
	<mn>5</mn>
</mfrac>
<mrow>
	<mi>x</mi>
	<mo>+</mo>
	<mfrac>
		<mn>7</mn>
		<mn>5</mn>
	</mfrac>
</mrow>
<mrow>
	<mi>y</mi>
	<mo>=</mo>
	<mfrac>
		<mn>2</mn>
		<mn>7</mn>
	</mfrac>
</mrow>
</math></p>
<p style="text-align: center;"><math alttext="g x plus k y equals five halves"><mrow>
	<mrow>
		<mrow>
			<mi>g</mi>
			<mi>x</mi>
		</mrow>
		<mo>+</mo>
		<mrow>
			<mi>k</mi>
			<mi>y</mi>
		</mrow>
	</mrow>
	<mo>=</mo>
	<mfrac>
		<mn>5</mn>
		<mn>2</mn>
	</mfrac>
</mrow>
</math></p>
<p style="text-align: left;">In the given system of equations, <math alttext="g"><mi>g</mi>
</math> and <math alttext="k"><mi>k</mi>
</math> are constants. The system has infinitely many solutions. What is the value of <math alttext="StartFraction g Over k EndFraction"><mrow>
	<mfrac>
		<mi>g</mi>
		<mi>k</mi>
	</mfrac>
</mrow>
</math>?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$[".2857","2/7"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$[".2857","2/7"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p style="text-align: left;">The correct answer is <math alttext="two sevenths"><mfrac>
<mn>2</mn>
<mn>7</mn>
</mfrac>
</math>. It&rsquo;s given that the system has infinitely many solutions. A system of two linear equations has infinitely many solutions if and only if the two linear equations are equivalent. Multiplying each side of the first equation in the system by <math alttext="StartFraction 35 Over 4 EndFraction"><mfrac>
<mn>35</mn>
<mn>4</mn>
</mfrac>
</math> yields <math alttext="StartFraction 35 Over 4 EndFraction left parenthesis two fifths x plus seven fifths y right parenthesis equals StartFraction 35 Over 4 EndFraction left parenthesis two sevenths right parenthesis"><mfrac><mn>35</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mfenced><mrow><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>5</mn></mfrac><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>7</mn><mn>5</mn></mfrac><mi>y</mi></mrow></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>35</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mfenced><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>7</mn></mfrac></mfenced></math>, or <math alttext="seven halves x plus StartFraction 49 Over 4 EndFraction y equals five halves"><mfrac><mn>7</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>49</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>5</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math>. Since this equation is equivalent to the second equation and has the same right side as the second equation, the coefficients of <math alttext="x"><mi>x</mi>
</math> and <math alttext="y"><mi>y</mi>
</math>, respectively, should also be the same. It follows that <math alttext="g equals seven halves"><mrow>
<mi>g</mi>
<mo>=</mo>
<mfrac>
<mn>7</mn>
<mn>2</mn>
</mfrac>
</mrow>
</math> and <math alttext="k equals StartFraction 49 Over 4 EndFraction"><mrow>
<mi>k</mi>
<mo>=</mo>
<mfrac>
<mn>49</mn>
<mn>4</mn>
</mfrac>
</mrow>
</math>. Therefore, the value of <math alttext="StartFraction g Over k EndFraction"><mrow>
<mfrac>
<mi>g</mi>
<mi>k</mi>
</mfrac>
</mrow>
</math> is <math alttext="StartStartFraction seven halves OverOver StartFraction 49 Over 4 EndFraction EndEndFraction"><mfrac><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>7</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>49</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mfrac></math>, or <math alttext="two sevenths"><mfrac>
<mn>2</mn>
<mn>7</mn>
</mfrac>
</math>. Note that 2/7, .2857, 0.285, and 0.286 are examples of ways to enter a correct answer.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"571174f3","external_id":"f55f83d0-fd97-488f-9f5c-014bc1e34d9c","disclosed_item_id":null,"source":"qbank","vaultid":null,"uId":"1a58f840-604a-48e7-9711-efba2d7f58ae","program":"SAT","test_type":2,"test_type_desc":"Math","primary_class_cd":"H","domain":"Algebra","skill_cd":"H.D.","skill_desc":"Systems of two linear equations in two variables","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":7,"type":"spr","stimulus":null,"stem":"<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><math alttext=\"two fifths x plus seven fifths y equals two sevenths\"><mfrac>\n\t<mn>2</mn>\n\t<mn>5</mn>\n</mfrac>\n<mrow>\n\t<mi>x</mi>\n\t<mo>+</mo>\n\t<mfrac>\n\t\t<mn>7</mn>\n\t\t<mn>5</mn>\n\t</mfrac>\n</mrow>\n<mrow>\n\t<mi>y</mi>\n\t<mo>=</mo>\n\t<mfrac>\n\t\t<mn>2</mn>\n\t\t<mn>7</mn>\n\t</mfrac>\n</mrow>\n</math></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><math alttext=\"g x plus k y equals five halves\"><mrow>\n\t<mrow>\n\t\t<mrow>\n\t\t\t<mi>g</mi>\n\t\t\t<mi>x</mi>\n\t\t</mrow>\n\t\t<mo>+</mo>\n\t\t<mrow>\n\t\t\t<mi>k</mi>\n\t\t\t<mi>y</mi>\n\t\t</mrow>\n\t</mrow>\n\t<mo>=</mo>\n\t<mfrac>\n\t\t<mn>5</mn>\n\t\t<mn>2</mn>\n\t</mfrac>\n</mrow>\n</math></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">In the given system of equations, <math alttext=\"g\"><mi>g</mi>\n</math> and <math alttext=\"k\"><mi>k</mi>\n</math> are constants. The system has infinitely many solutions. What is the value of <math alttext=\"StartFraction g Over k EndFraction\"><mrow>\n\t<mfrac>\n\t\t<mi>g</mi>\n\t\t<mi>k</mi>\n\t</mfrac>\n</mrow>\n</math>?</p>","answerOptions":[],"keys":[".2857","2/7"],"correct_answer":[".2857","2/7"],"rationale":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The correct answer is <math alttext=\"two sevenths\"><mfrac>\n<mn>2</mn>\n<mn>7</mn>\n</mfrac>\n</math>. It&rsquo;s given that the system has infinitely many solutions. A system of two linear equations h